div.p:
The first two arguments support Aristotle's thesis that friends are essential, but the second only serve to convince us that they are desirable.  As such, we might read this section as rather a set of arguments for a weaker claim-why we ought to have friends-rather than the stronger claim of necessity.  However, Aristotle does not describe the good life as the

 best possible life for an individual;

it is rather that life which is most desirable-the highest human good.  On this interpretation, it is possible understand Aristotle's two arguments for great desirability as arguments for necessity.
